W:Let me tell you the story
W:Of a man named Charlie
W:On a tragic and fateful day
W:He put ten cents in his pocket,
W:Kissed his wife and family
W:Went to ride on the MTA
W:
W: Charlie handed in his dime
W:At the Kendall Square Station
W:And he changed for Jamaica Plain
W:When he got there the conductor told him,
W:"One more nickel."
W:Charlie could not get off that train.
W:
W:Did he ever return,
W:No he never returned
W:And his fate is still unlearn'd
W:He may ride forever
W:'neath the streets of Boston
W:He's the man who never returned.
W:
W:W: Now all night long
W:Charlie rides through the tunnels
W:Saying, "What will become of me?
W:Crying
W:How can I afford to see
W:My sister in Chelsea
W:Or my cousin in Roxbury?"
W:
W:W: Charlie's wife goes down
W:To the Scollay Square station
W:Every day at quarter past two
W:And through the open window
W:She hands Charlie a sandwich
W:As the train comes rumblin' through.
W:
W:W: As his train rolled on
W:underneath Greater Boston
W:Charlie looked around and sighed:
W:"Well, I'm sore and disgusted
W:And I'm absolutely busted;
W:I guess this is my last long ride."
